es  are ever more critical  but for the ukrainian troops holding on  in the donbas the weapons aren't coming  fast enough  well let's go to dw correspondent  emmanuel shaz who's in kiev good to see  you emmanuel now volatility has traveled  to the southern front line for the first  time  how important is this for ukrainians to  see their president leaving the capital  well vladimir zelansky is the president  of all of ukrainians not just people  based in care for people who have  had to flee the country following this  war and he is very much proving it uh by  this visit on the southern front uh  which has been under  nearly constant shelling which has  suffered a heavy fighting and of course  heavy losses for the ukrainian army you  have to keep in mind that there's at  least 100 ukrainian soldiers maybe much  more dying on a daily  basis so i think it's quite important  that the president is coming to visit  the troops for uh to boost their morale  and also he was handing out some medals  to  to some soldiers who had been injured  but also for civilians it's important  that they don't only see their president  giving addresses from the presidential  palace post  posting his addresses on social media  it's important for ukrainians to see  that the president is very much hands-on  and is pretty pretty much on their side  because you know there's a huge  difference uh in this war uh about what  is happening in the eastern front and on  the southern front compared to places  that feel relatively safer like here in  kiev life is of course not normal  because everybody is very much aware  there's a war going on but we're not  under constant challenges just like in  other parts of the country so very  significant visit today  now nato general secretary jens  stoltenberg told a german newspaper  today that the war in ukraine could last  years how much of a blow is this for  ukrainians and is there much discussion  on what that might look like for ukraine  um what really u